AB  - Metal inert gas (MIG) weld defect detection is critical for ensuring safety and reliability in modern manufacturing. However, the complexity of industrial environments and the scarcity of labeled defect data present significant challenges. Traditional detection approaches, which heavily rely on supervised learning, often suffer performance degradation due to limited annotations. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a two-stage detection framework specifically designed for MIG weld defects. The method integrates masked image modeling with Local-MIM (local multi-scale reconstruction) for self-supervised feature learning, and utilizes the real-time detection transformer (RT-DETR) model for efficient object detection. In the pre-training stage, Local-MIM autonomously captures latent defect features from unlabeled weld images. These features are subsequently utilized by the RT-DETR model to achieve accurate and real-time defect localization. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed approach attains exceptional detection accuracy under limited supervision, while exhibiting strong robustness and inference speed. Furthermore, the introduction of the High-Low attention mechanism significantly enhances the extraction of low-level features without incurring additional computational costs. This study presents a novel and practical solution for MIG weld quality control, with promising potential for industrial deployment.
