AB  - High-power laser–arc hybrid welding (HPLAHW) is widely used for joining medium-thick plates. A challenging issue in HPLAHW is welding process penetration depth monitoring, because strong arc and laser interaction both hinders the application of internal information sensors such as optical coherence tomography (OCT) and disturbs external signals. To enable nondestructive, in-process penetration depth monitoring in HPLAHW, this research proposes a lightweight digital twin multi-fidelity and multi-sensor deep learning framework centered on the designed MFMSNet for real-time in-process penetration depth monitoring in HPLAHW. In the perception field, top-view welding images and plasma emission spectra are acquired to characterize arc fluctuation, keyhole visibility, droplet transfer, and radiation-related process responses. In the physical field, computational fluid dynamics (CFD)-derived keyhole depth is introduced to provide internal physical information associated with penetration formation. A digital twin dataset is constructed through image and spectral denoising, data resampling, and temporal alignment to reduce periodic pulsed arc-induced optical noise, arc–keyhole overlap, and periodic vapor plume ejection in HPLAHW. Furthermore, a lightweight multi-branch temporal fusion network is developed, in which compact modality-specific encoders equipped with attention-based feature selection and recurrent temporal modeling extract penetration-sensitive visual spatiotemporal patterns, spectral radiation dynamics, and CFD-derived keyhole evolution features from multi-sensor and multi-fidelity digital twin data. The fused temporal features are used to generate continuous, time-resolved penetration depth monitoring along the welding process, rather than a single weld-level scalar output. Experimental results show that the proposed method outperforms the other evaluated model structures and fusion configurations, achieving an MAE of 0.28 mm and an RMSE of 0.34 mm, which correspond to 2.8% and 3.4% of the 10 mm plate thickness, respectively, demonstrating that the proposed digital twin framework improves the stability and reliability of in-process penetration depth monitoring.
