AB  - This chapter introduces graphics processing unit (GPU) computing fundamentals and parallel programming models (CUDA and OpenACC), highlighting their architectures, workflows, and use cases in HPC. It begins with a comparison of central processing unit versus GPU architectures and traces GPU evolution from graphics accelerators to general‑purpose processors with streaming multiprocessors and hierarchical memory. CUDA is presented in detail: its thread‑block‑grid execution model, memory management (cudaMalloc, cudaMemcpy), synchronization (__syncthreads, atomic operations), and kernel-launch configuration, illustrated by vector addition and matrix multiplication examples. OpenACC’s directive‑based approach is then explained, showing how simple pragmas (parallel loop, data, collapse) enable rapid code offload to accelerators with minimal code changes. The chapter compares CUDA’s fine‑grained control and peak performance against OpenACC’s ease of use and portability and explores hybrid Message Passing Interface+OpenACC and Heterogeneous System Architecture unified‑memory strategies. Finally, it covers GPU optimization techniques, coalesced accesses, shared memory, occupancy tuning, and profiling/debugging tools like Nsight Compute and cuda‑gdb, with real‑world examples in weather simulation and deep learning.
