AB  - Automated weld bead inspection in nuclear reprocessing plants is critical for ensuring structural integrity, yet has remained challenging due to extreme environmental conditions including complex geometries and significant noise interference. To address these challenges, this study proposed the Reprocessing Weld Bead Net (RWB-Net), a novel deep learning framework consisting of three methodological innovations. First, a light-robust deformed denoising neural network was proposed to suppress environmental noise while preserving essential defect features, yielding a 5.22 % performance improvement in controlled experiments. Second, for accurate weld bead localization, an attention U-Net enhanced with hidden Markov model optimization was developed, achieving a mean intersection over union of 91.43 % on intricate nuclear pipeline geometries—surpassing standard U-Net models by 12.6 %. Third, defect classification was performed using a multi-source feature fusion strategy that integrated 182 domain-specific handcrafted features with deep representations from VGG-19, resulting in a classification accuracy of 94.26 % across four defect types. Comparative evaluations demonstrated RWB-Net's superior performance, with a recall of 93.72 %, F1-score of 93.66 %, and efficient inference speed of 0.41 s per image. The primary contribution of this work lay in the synergistic integration of domain expertise with advanced data-driven approaches tailored to the nuclear context. Cross-dataset validation confirmed RWB-Net's robustness and real-time applicability for weld quality assessment in safety-critical environments, offering insights extendable to broader industrial inspection domains.
